---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="OAuth">
  <h1>OAuth</h1>
  <p>
    Identity supports Google, Microsoft, GitHub, and Apple as upstream OAuth
    providers, plus any standards-compliant OIDC provider added purely
    through config. The flow is server-owned: the client calls
    <code>BeginOAuthLogin</code>, redirects the browser to the returned
    provider URL, then completes the callback with <code>OAuthLogin</code>.
    Identity does the provider token exchange, verifies the upstream
    identity, upserts the local user, and issues its own access and
    refresh tokens.
  </p>

  <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>Security model.</strong> <code>BeginOAuthLogin</code> mints a
    signed state token plus a PKCE verifier. <code>OAuthLogin</code>
    rejects callbacks whose returned <code>state</code> does not match the
    server-minted token, so CSRF protection does not depend on a
    client-side state cache.
  </div>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>Configure each provider you intend to support:</p>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=common
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_APPLE_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_APPLE_TEAM_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_APPLE_KEY_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY="..."`} lang="bash" />

  <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm mt-4">
    <strong>Apple Configuration.</strong> Apple requires a Team ID, a Services ID (used as the Client ID), a Key ID, and an ECDSA private key (PEM or Base64).
    Unlike other providers, Apple sends this headless flow's callback via <code>POST</code>. Configure an application-owned HTTPS callback that accepts <code>code</code>, <code>state</code>, and the optional <code>user</code> payload, then forwards them to <code>OAuthLogin</code>. Configure <code>https://your-api.com/oauth/callback/apple</code> only when using the hosted flow below.
  </div>

  <h3>Generic OIDC provider</h3>
  <p>
    Register an arbitrary standards-compliant OIDC provider (Okta, Auth0,
    Keycloak, a self-hosted issuer) without a code release. Identity resolves
    the endpoints from the issuer's
    <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> document, verifies the
    id_token against the discovered JWKS (issuer, audience, expiry, signature),
    and rejects any user whose email is not verified.
  </p>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_ENABLED=true
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_PROVIDER_KEY=okta
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_ISSUER=https://acme.okta.com
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=...
-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_SCOPES="openid email profile"`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The provider registers under <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_PROVIDER_KEY</code>
    (which becomes the reported <code>provider</code> and the value passed to
    <code>BeginOAuthLogin</code> / <code>OAuthLogin</code>) and flows through the
    same hosted and headless paths as every built-in provider. Override the
    discovery URL with <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL</code>. The key
    may not be <code>google</code>, <code>microsoft</code>, <code>github</code>,
    or <code>apple</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Per-project providers (the Firebase-project model)</h2>
  <p>
    A Project is identity's isolation container — the Firebase-project
    equivalent. Each project configures its <strong>own</strong> OAuth providers,
    exactly like enabling providers in a Firebase project's Auth console, and one
    project never sees another's. The <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_*</code> environment
    variables above configure the <strong>default project's</strong> providers
    only; existing single-project deployments are unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Precedence, per provider, for the request's resolved project:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      if the project has that provider in its <code>config_json</code> →
      it is used (its secret is decrypted at build time);
    </li>
    <li>
      else if the project <strong>is</strong> the default project
      (<code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID</code>) → the env-configured provider is
      used;
    </li>
    <li>
      else — a non-default project with no such provider — the provider is
      <strong>unavailable</strong> for that project (the same
      "unknown provider" error an unregistered provider returns). A non-default
      project never inherits the default project's providers, so credentials
      never leak across projects.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A project's providers live under an <code>"oauth"</code> object in its
    <code>config_json</code> (the same per-project blob that carries branding,
    passkey RP-ID, and CORS), keyed by provider. Provider secrets are stored
    <strong>encrypted at rest</strong> (<code>client_secret_enc</code>, Apple
    <code>private_key_enc</code>) with AES-256-GCM:
  </p>
  <Code code={`{
  "oauth": {
    "google":    { "client_id": "...", "client_secret_enc": "<enc>", "native_audiences": ["web.g.apps.googleusercontent.com", "ios.g"] },
    "microsoft": { "client_id": "...", "client_secret_enc": "<enc>", "tenant_id": "", "native_audiences": ["ms-native-client"] },
    "apple":     { "native_audiences": ["com.example.app"] },
    "oidc":      { "client_id": "...", "client_secret_enc": "<enc>", "issuer": "https://acme.okta.com", "scopes": "openid email profile" }
  }
}`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    Each Google/Apple/Microsoft block drives the <strong>hosted</strong> flow
    (via the credentials) and the <strong>native</strong> flow (via
    <code>native_audiences</code>) independently — a block may configure hosted,
    native, or both. The Apple block above is <em>native-only</em> (no hosted
    credentials). See <strong>Native mobile sign-in</strong> below for how
    <code>native_audiences</code> is resolved per project.
  </p>
  <p>
    The encryption key is a base64-encoded 32-byte key supplied via
    <code>GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY</code>. It is <strong>required</strong>
    whenever the postgres control plane is enabled
    (<code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres</code>), because only postgres stores a
    non-default project's credentials; drivers without a control plane pin every
    request to the default project and need no key. Rotating or losing the key
    invalidates every stored provider secret. GitHub is not part of the
    per-project schema — it remains an env-only provider for the default project.
  </p>
  <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>Upgrade note.</strong> This key is <strong>mandatory</strong> for
    postgres deployments (<code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres</code>) — boot fails
    fast without it, so existing deployments must set it before upgrading.
    Generate one with:
    <Code code={`openssl rand -base64 32`} lang="bash" />
  </div>
  <p>
    Built exchangers (and their JWKS/discovery caches) are cached per
    <code>(project, provider, config)</code>, so a config edit rebuilds while
    steady-state logins reuse the cache.
  </p>

  <h2>Authoring a project's providers (admin API)</h2>
  <p>
    You never hand-craft the encrypted <code>*_enc</code> fields. Three
    operator-only admin RPCs author a project's providers — the operator sends
    the secret in <strong>plaintext over TLS</strong>, and the server encrypts it
    with <code>GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY</code> before storing it. They are
    authenticated by the <code>X-Admin-Secret</code> header (like every other
    <code>Admin*</code> RPC), and are <code>UNIMPLEMENTED</code> on a driver with
    no control plane.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>AdminSetProjectOAuthProvider</code> — set or rotate one provider.
      The write carries the non-secret fields plus a write-only plaintext
      <code>client_secret</code> (or Apple <code>apple_private_key</code>). An
      <strong>empty</strong> secret on a subsequent write keeps the stored one,
      so you can edit non-secret fields without re-sending it. The provider block
      is merged into <code>config_json</code> without disturbing any other key
      (branding, passkey, CORS, login), and the merged config is validated at
      author time (e.g. a Microsoft <code>issuer_format</code> must contain
      exactly one <code>%s</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>AdminDeleteProjectOAuthProvider</code> — remove one provider
      (idempotent).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>AdminListProjectOAuthProviders</code> — list configured providers.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Reads never return secrets.</strong> The set response and the list
    response redact all secret material, reporting only
    <code>has_client_secret</code> / <code>has_private_key</code> booleans.
  </p>
  <Code code={`message ProjectOAuthProviderConfig {
  string provider = 1;              // "google" | "microsoft" | "apple" | "oidc"
  string client_id = 2;
  string client_secret = 3;         // write-only plaintext; empty = keep stored
  bool   has_client_secret = 4;     // read-only (redacted presence)
  repeated string native_audiences = 5;
  // google endpoint overrides (6-9), microsoft tenant_id/issuer_format (10-11),
  // apple team_id/key_id/apple_private_key (12-14, key is write-only),
  // has_private_key (15), oidc issuer/discovery_url/scopes (16-18).
}

rpc AdminSetProjectOAuthProvider(AdminSetProjectOAuthProviderRequest) returns (AdminSetProjectOAuthProviderResponse);
rpc AdminDeleteProjectOAuthProvider(AdminDeleteProjectOAuthProviderRequest) returns (AdminDeleteProjectOAuthProviderResponse);
rpc AdminListProjectOAuthProviders(AdminListProjectOAuthProvidersRequest) returns (AdminListProjectOAuthProvidersResponse);`} lang="protobuf" />
  <Code code={`# Author a project's Google provider (secret sent in plaintext over TLS).
curl -sS https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/AdminSetProjectOAuthProvider \\
  -H "X-Admin-Secret: $GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "project_id": "proj_123",
    "config": {
      "provider": "google",
      "client_id": "1234.apps.googleusercontent.com",
      "client_secret": "GOCSPX-plaintext-secret"
    }
  }'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Start RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message BeginOAuthLoginRequest {
  string redirect_uri = 1;
  string provider     = 2;  // "google", "microsoft", "github", or "apple"
  string tenant       = 3;  // Microsoft tenant ID (optional)
}

message BeginOAuthLoginResponse {
  string authorization_url = 1;
  string state             = 2;
  string state_token       = 3;
  string code_verifier     = 4;
  int32 expires_in         = 5;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Callback RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`message OAuthLoginRequest {
  string code          = 1;  // authorization code from provider
  string redirect_uri  = 2;
  string provider      = 3;  // "google", "microsoft", "github", or "apple"
  string code_verifier = 4;  // optional when state_token is present
  string tenant        = 5;  // Microsoft tenant ID (optional)
  string state         = 6;  // callback state from the provider redirect
  string state_token   = 7;  // opaque token minted by BeginOAuthLogin
  string apple_user_payload = 8; // one-time user payload from Apple's form_post callback
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Response</h2>
  <Code code={`message OAuthLoginResponse {
  User user             = 1;
  string access_token   = 2;  // backend-issued JWT
  string refresh_token  = 4;
  int32 expires_in      = 5;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Native mobile sign-in</h2>
  <p>
    Mobile apps that use the platform account picker (the official
    <code>google_sign_in</code> / <code>sign_in_with_apple</code> Flutter
    plugins) obtain an <strong>ID token</strong> directly — no browser, no
    PKCE. <code>NativeOAuthLogin</code> verifies that ID token server-side and
    issues backend tokens, scoped to the product's project. It is public
    (auth-exempt) and rate-limited under the per-IP login quota, exactly like
    <code>OAuthLogin</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`message NativeOAuthLoginRequest {
  string provider = 1;  // "google" | "apple" | "microsoft"
  string id_token = 2;  // GoogleSignInAuthentication.idToken / Apple identityToken / MSAL id_token
  string product  = 3;  // product selector → project (see config below)
  string nonce    = 4;  // Apple: RAW nonce (hashed); Microsoft: verbatim nonce (both optional)
}

message NativeOAuthLoginResponse {
  User user            = 1;
  string access_token  = 2;
  string refresh_token = 3;
  int32 expires_in     = 4;
}`} lang="proto" />
  <p>
    <strong>Verification.</strong> Google: the token's signature is checked
    against Google's certs, <code>iss</code> must be
    <code>accounts.google.com</code> (with or without the <code>https://</code>
    prefix), <code>exp</code> must be present and in the future,
    <code>email_verified</code> must be true, and <code>aud</code> must be an
    accepted Google audience for the request's project (see
    <strong>Audience scoping</strong> below). Apple: signature against Apple's
    JWKS, <code>iss = https://appleid.apple.com</code>, a present, unexpired
    <code>exp</code>, and an accepted Apple <code>aud</code>. Hide-my-email relay
    addresses are accepted as-is. Microsoft: signature against the
    common-endpoint JWKS, a present <code>tid</code> (tenant), an
    <code>iss</code> equal to
    <code>https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tid&gt;/v2.0</code> (multi-tenant
    by default — the issuer is derived from the token's own <code>tid</code>; a
    project may pin <code>tenant_id</code> / <code>issuer_format</code> to accept
    one tenant only), a present unexpired <code>exp</code>, an accepted
    <code>aud</code>, and an email coalesced from
    <code>email → preferred_username → upn</code> (subject is <code>oid</code>
    then <code>sub</code>). Any failure returns <code>Unauthenticated</code> with
    a safe message.
  </p>
  <div class="rounded-lg border border-red-500/40 bg-red-500/10 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>🔒 Security — multi-tenant Microsoft &amp; account linking.</strong>
    Native (and hosted) Microsoft login defaults to <strong>multi-tenant</strong>:
    the expected issuer is derived from the token's own <code>tid</code>, so
    <strong>any</strong> Azure AD tenant — including one an attacker controls —
    can mint a valid token. Because a token carries an <code>email</code>, this is
    an <a href="https://www.descope.com/blog/post/noauth" rel="nofollow">nOAuth</a>-style
    account-takeover vector when email is trusted for cross-provider linking: an
    attacker can present a Microsoft token bearing a <strong>victim's email</strong>
    and take over the account. For email-based linking,
    <strong>pin <code>tenant_id</code></strong> (single-tenant) on the project's
    <code>oauth.microsoft</code> block unless you fully trust every tenant that
    can obtain a token. Do <strong>not</strong> trust a multi-tenant Microsoft
    email for cross-provider account linking. (This matches the existing hosted
    Microsoft provider; a tenant allowlist and the <code>xms_edov</code>
    email-verified claim are planned hardening for both flows.)
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Audience scoping (per-project).</strong> The accepted <code>aud</code>
    values are resolved <strong>per project</strong> from its <code>config_json</code>
    — <code>oauth.google.native_audiences</code>,
    <code>oauth.apple.native_audiences</code>, and
    <code>oauth.microsoft.native_audiences</code> (each an array of client ids /
    bundle ids). A non-default project accepts only its own configured audiences
    and never inherits the env seed, so a token minted for one project's client
    id is rejected under another. The <strong>default project</strong> falls back
    to the env seed <code>GATEWAY_NATIVE_OAUTH_GOOGLE_AUDIENCES</code> /
    <code>GATEWAY_NATIVE_OAUTH_APPLE_AUDIENCES</code> /
    <code>GATEWAY_NATIVE_OAUTH_MICROSOFT_AUDIENCES</code> when it configures none
    of its own; if it configures its own, config_json wins and the env seed is
    not merged in. A provider with no audiences for the resolved project cannot
    be used natively.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Single-use replay protection.</strong> A successfully verified
    native ID token is redeemed <strong>once</strong>: the token's identifier
    (its <code>jti</code>, or a stable digest of its claims when the provider
    stamps none) is recorded in a repo-backed replay cache, and a second
    presentation of the same token is rejected until the record expires (bounded
    by the token's own <code>exp</code>). This is enforced server-side for every
    provider, in addition to the optional nonce below. Because audiences are now
    per-project, a token is valid for exactly one project, so single-use is not
    weakened by any cross-project audience overlap.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Nonce (per-provider difference).</strong> The <code>nonce</code>
    field is <strong>optional but recommended</strong>; the check runs only when
    the client sends a raw nonce. <em>Apple</em> HASHES it: the server verifies
    the id_token's <code>nonce</code> claim equals
    <code>hex(SHA-256(raw nonce))</code> — the <code>sign_in_with_apple</code> /
    Firebase convention (<code>base64url(SHA-256(raw))</code> is also accepted).
    <em>Microsoft</em> echoes the nonce <strong>verbatim</strong>: the claim must
    equal the raw nonce exactly (no hashing). <em>Google</em> does not use the
    nonce. A missing or mismatched claim (when a nonce was supplied) is rejected;
    when omitted, the token is still fully verified and remains single-use.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Product → project.</strong> <code>product</code> is mapped to an
    identity project via
    <code>GATEWAY_NATIVE_OAUTH_PRODUCT_PROJECTS</code> (CSV of
    <code>product=projectID</code>); a product that isn't listed is treated as a
    project id directly. With a control plane (Postgres) the project must exist
    and be active, otherwise the request is <code>InvalidArgument</code>; on a
    single-project deployment only the product that resolves to the default
    project is accepted. The resolved project is injected as the request's
    project scope, so account linking and token issuance happen under it —
    independent of the request Host. When
    <code>GATEWAY_NATIVE_OAUTH_ENABLED</code> is off the RPC returns
    <code>FailedPrecondition</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Account linking reuses the same path as hosted OAuth (match on
    <code>(provider, sub)</code>, then email; create if new), so the
    <a href="#upsert-semantics">upsert semantics</a> below apply identically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="upsert-semantics">Upsert semantics</h2>
  <p>
    On a successful OAuth verification, Identity resolves the user by
    <code>(provider, provider_user_id)</code> first, then falls back to the
    provider email for first-time linking:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, active.</strong> Tokens are issued; profile
      fields (<code>name</code>, <code>avatar_url</code>) are updated from
      the OAuth claims.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, invited.</strong> The login is rejected with
      <code>FailedPrecondition</code> ("accept your invitation first")
      until they accept the invitation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Existing user, deactivated/suspended.</strong> Rejected with
      <code>FailedPrecondition</code> ("account is &lt;status&gt;").
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>New user.</strong> Created with status
      <code>USER_STATUS_ACTIVE</code> and role <code>member</code>.
      No password hash is stored — subsequent <code>PasswordLogin</code>
      attempts return <code>no password set for this account</code> until
      they set one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>OAuth + password coexistence</h2>
  <p>
    A user created via OAuth has no password hash, but can opt in by going
    through <code>RequestPasswordReset</code> → email link →
    <code>AcceptInvitation</code>-style setup, or by an admin's
    <code>ResetUserPassword</code>. After that they can log in with either
    provider.
  </p>

  <h2>Duplicate-link safety: project-scoped unique index</h2>
  <p>
    The link between a local user and a provider-side identity is stored
    as an <code>OAuthIdentity</code> row. Uniqueness is enforced by a
    project-scoped three-column SQL unique index on
    <code>(project_id, provider, provider_user_id)</code>
    (<code>oi_project_provider_subject_uidx</code>), so the storage layer
    guarantees "at most one local user per provider-side identity within a
    project" atomically — two concurrent <code>OAuthLogin</code> calls
    that race to link the same Google account onto two different local
    accounts collide on that real unique index in Postgres, and exactly
    one of them succeeds. The memory driver mirrors the same constraint,
    returning <code>oauth identity already linked: &lt;provider&gt;/&lt;sub&gt;</code>
    on a conflict.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosted OAuth flow (Firebase-style)</h2>
  <p>
    On top of the headless RPCs above, identity also exposes a fully
    server-owned OAuth flow that handles the provider redirects and
    callbacks for you. Two plain HTTP routes (not Connect-RPC) drive
    it — the browser is 302-redirected through them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>GET /oauth/start/{`{provider}`}?return_to=...</code> — mints
      the state token, builds the provider authorization URL, and
      redirects the browser to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GET/POST /oauth/callback/{`{provider}`}</code> — receives the
      provider's authorization code (via URL query or POST form body), exchanges it through the same
      <code>OAuthLogin</code> internals, mints an opaque single-use code
      (<code>OAuthOneTimeCode</code>, 60s TTL), and 302-redirects to
      <code>return_to?code=&lt;otc&gt;</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The SPA then exchanges the one-time code for tokens via the
    <code>RedeemOAuthCode</code> RPC. No token material is persisted at
    rest — only the user id is stored alongside the
    <code>code_hash</code>, so a leaked code store is useless after the
    60s window or a single redeem.
  </p>

  <h3>Enabling the hosted flow</h3>
  <p>
    The hosted flow is <strong>off by default</strong>. Enable it by
    setting an allowlist of <code>return_to</code> origins or origin-bound path prefixes:
  </p>
  <Code code={`-e GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS=https://app.example.com,https://staging.example.com`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    A <code>return_to</code> must match an allowlist origin. A path entry
    permits that path and its descendants; anything else is rejected with HTTP 400 at
    <code>/oauth/start</code> before any provider round-trip. Empty (the
    default) disables the routes entirely — they return 404 and only the
    headless RPCs work. The single provider-facing redirect URI registered
    with each provider is
    <code>&lt;identity-origin&gt;/oauth/callback/&lt;provider&gt;</code>,
    so you configure it once per provider and reuse it across all your
    SPA origins (which appear only in the <code>return_to</code>
    allowlist, not at the provider).
  </p>

  <h3>End-to-end browser walkthrough</h3>
  <Code code={`// 1. SPA kicks off the flow by sending the user to /oauth/start.
//    No state-token round-trip on the client; identity owns it.
window.location.href =
  "https://auth.example.com/oauth/start/google" +
  "?return_to=" + encodeURIComponent("https://app.example.com/oauth/done");

// 2. Provider redirects back to /oauth/callback/google with ?code=...
//    Identity exchanges, links/creates the OAuthIdentity row, mints
//    a one-time code, and redirects to return_to with ?code=otc_...

// 3. /oauth/done in the SPA: exchange the otc for real tokens.
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const otc = params.get("code");
const { user, accessToken, refreshToken } = await client.redeemOAuthCode({
  code: otc,
});

storeTokens(accessToken, refreshToken);
window.location.replace("/dashboard");`} lang="typescript" />
  <p>
    See decision log §10 in <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/blob/main/docs/IDENTITY.md"><code>docs/IDENTITY.md</code></a>
    for the design rationale (why a one-time code rather than a
    fragment-token or cookie, why <code>return_to</code> is a separate
    signed artifact rather than a state-token claim, and why the
    allowlist is fail-closed).
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
